{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791617007,"nonce":"2oc0kLLmbNvRxpOaUKxihdcKwEU=","title":"cfg_simplify rescans the function after every merge and every threaded block","message":"cfg_simplify::run looked for the first mergeable pair from the top of the block list after each merge, and for the first threadable block after each threading, and removed each absorbed or threaded block with IndexMap::shift_remove, which moves every block after it: O(blocks) per step, so O(blocks squared) for a function with many. method_call's lua$chunk merges 282 blocks and threads 263 in its first optimisation round (2.1 ms).\n\nFix: one walk in block order merges every chain into its head (merging is confluent: a chain ends as its head whichever pair merges first); threading walks in block order and repeats until a walk threads nothing; absorbed and threaded blocks are emptied in place and leave together through one retain, which keeps the order of the rest.\n\nChecked: optimised bodies of 14 Lua benchmarks with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 are byte-identical; Python go, richards, raytrace, deltablue and chaos bodies are identical once ids and pointer constants are masked (they differ that way between two runs of the old build too). Start-up instructions (llvm-backend build, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3, Mac): Lua 25.40G -\u003e 25.11G (-1.2%; method_call -4.2%, op_overload -1.2%); Python flat.","files":null}]}